virglrenderer: H.264 and HEVC encoding on the Mac's media engine (VideoToolbox)

What: the VideoToolbox video backend (virgl-videotoolbox-decode.patch) also
encodes. The host offers H.264 Constrained Baseline, Main and High and HEVC Main
with the ENCODE entrypoint (16x16 up to 4096x2304), each only when VideoToolbox
has a hardware encoder for it; OMACVM_VIDEO_NO_ENCODE=1 leaves encoding out
(OMACVM_VIDEO_NO_HEVC=1 HEVC; the guest then encodes in software). Per frame:
begin_frame takes a picture from the compression session's pool and vrend
copies the guest's NV12 plane textures into it (GPU blit into the IOSurface
planes, CPU readback as fallback); encode_bitstream applies the guest's
bitrate, peak, frame rate, GOP or constant QP (all clamped) and forces a key
frame for IDR pictures; end_frame waits for the frame and hands vrend an Annex B
access unit (parameter sets before every key frame). The stream has the size
the guest's SPS crops its padded pictures to (1920x1088 -> 1080; the session is
made with the first picture description, the first picture copied once).
Real time, no frame reordering (no B frames). Bitrate modes: no low-latency
rate control (it drops frames, which a VA-API encoder cannot report; Chrome
then gives up on the hardware encoder); DataRateLimits (1.5 x the target per
second) keeps the rate instead. Constant QP: a low-latency session that takes
the guest's I/P QP with every frame (BaseFrameQP, no rate control, nothing
dropped); an encoder without per-frame QP gets VideoToolbox's quality from the
QP instead (an approximation, logged). A switch between bitrate and constant
QP makes a new session, so no setting of the other mode stays. A frame that
fails, does not fit, or whose picture could not be copied is reported to
the guest as a failure, never cut or left with the previous frame's feedback.
Threading: VideoToolbox's output callback may run on its own thread at any time
after EncodeFrame; the coded data and status are under a lock, a frame the
guest never ended is completed, dropped and reported as failed when the next
one begins, and time stamps add up per frame (they keep increasing when the
frame rate changes).
vrend_video.c: the planes must be R8/RG8 textures (GL errors in the copy fail
it), the upload reports whether the picture was copied (virgl_video.h:
encode_upload_picture returns int); coded-data and feedback resources must be
buffers and stay
referenced from encode_bitstream to the end of the frame (a guest could unref
them in between); coded data that does not fit the guest's buffer, or a buffer
that cannot be mapped, gives failure feedback (was: a cut frame reported as
success, and a NULL memcpy). A frame refused before it reaches the encoder (no
such codec, picture, description or coded-data resource, or one that is not a
buffer) also gets failure feedback, and so does a second encode in one frame
(the first keeps its buffers and gets its result). Not covered: a feedback
resource that is missing or not a buffer (nothing to write to), and a frame
still open when the guest destroys its codec.
Why: WebRTC (camera, screen sharing) and FFmpeg h264_vaapi/hevc_vaapi in the VM
encode on the CPU otherwise.
Upstream: OmacVM addition to its own backend; the vrend_video.c fixes are
upstreamable.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-video-encode.c, for H.264 and HEVC: 30 frames 320x240
through the virgl video protocol (feedback, parameter sets, IDR, decode back
with VideoToolbox, luma PSNR > 30 dB), nonsense and random picture
descriptions, a too small, a non-buffer and a missing coded resource (failure
feedback), refused sizes and profiles, cropped pictures through the GPU blit
and the CPU copy, frame-rate changes, a frame never ended (failure feedback,
no leak into the next), two encodes in one frame, failure feedback, constant
QP 18 against QP 40 and against 50 kbit/s before and after it (the size
follows the QP). In a VM: tests/video/
(FFmpeg, Chrome WebRTC camera, screen and calls).
